she's one of a new wave of female  independents targeting seats held by  australia's ruling coalition party  frustrated by the two-party stalemate on  climate change the  journalist-turned-politician says people  voting in the election this weekend want  policies backed by science and immediate  action i think the reason that many of  the independents have focused on climate  is because that's what the australian  people want they want faster better more  focused economic action on climate  policy and they haven't got it  australia is one of the highest emitters  of greenhouse gases in the world per  capita and is the second largest  exporter of coal  it's also one of the nation's most at  risk from climate change  a scientific study warns australia will  become uninsurable by the end of this  decade unless emissions are drastically  reduced flooding was the most common  risk followed by bushfires  climate change is already hitting many  australian communities very hard one  area we find this is it's becoming  harder for people to insure their homes  some recent research we did show that by  2030 one in 25 australian properties  could be effectively uninsurable higher  than average temperatures have again  caused severe bleaching in parts of the  great barrier reef  next month unesco will assess whether  the australian government has done  enough to address threats to the coral  with the world heritage committee  considering listing it as in danger  now the great barrier reef has just  experienced its sixth mass bleaching  event due to marine heat have been  exacerbated by climate change and it's  disappointing because the current  administration is not taking this  seriously  the government and the opposition have  committed to a zero net emission target  by 2050 both major parties continue to  support fossil fuels with more than 100  new coal and gas projects on the  government's official register climate  scientists say their policies fall short  of what's needed to limit warming to  below two degrees  the largest voter survey in australia  found that at least one third of people  consider climate change as the most  important issue in this election and  what action to reduce emissions by 2030  but some coal towns lie in swing  constituencies and it's those  electorates that are key to winning this  election sarah clark al jazeera sydney  australia
 

